-called peace agreement in the  gulf region and the arab world  that it wants to show that it that it  demands respect that it wants to be  treated as equal  and on the israeli side um there's also  an understanding with the united states  that  when such deals happen is the united  states should get a green light  from israel which is quite outstanding  when you think about it but this goes  back  some 40 years now you know probably a  lot of our viewers don't remember but  the united states wanted to sell  sophisticated reconnaissance planes  called a wax back in 1981  40 years ago and israel objected and  then they went on to this  whole hoopla crisis between the two  capitals  until the united states warned the day  but also israel got a lot in return and  i think that's what netanyahu is trying  to do this time around  he's maneuvering to say that he opposes  the deal so that  when the deal passes he will get a lot  in return from the united states  but still that's still there's there's a  question about  why does the uae needs f-35s  to kill more yemenis to  bomb iran because if that really happens  i mean on the yemeni side of course we  will know the disaster of the war there  and  any conflict with iran will mean that  abu dhabi and dubai  you know could be forgotten about  so any which way you look at this  controversy and what's behind it  it's kind of all to be honest stinks  let me ask you a moment about the  involvement in iran in all of this  because we were mentioning earlier that  relations between iran and the uae do  seem to to be strengthening at least  that are long-standing links there  it is interesting to note that they  managed to try and get this deal done  knowing that uae does have those links  with iran and of course as we mentioned  that israel  is diametrically opposed to iran it  seems very difficult to see how this  deal is actually going to work in  practice  i think the the deal is not viewed  positively in tehran there's no doubt